1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,685
Sodium is stored under kerosene.

2
00:00:02,685 --> 00:00:05,512
Chlorine is used to clean drinking water.

3
00:00:05,512 --> 00:00:08,827
Neon glows in a sign,
but almost never reacts.

4
00:00:09,227 --> 00:00:12,593
Three elements with three
very different personalities.

5
00:00:12,593 --> 00:00:14,719
Yet one map predicts all three.

6
00:00:15,169 --> 00:00:19,056
Aarav stops at the periodic table
on his college notice board.

7
00:00:19,406 --> 00:00:24,225
Sodium sits far left, chlorine near
the far right, neon at the very end.

8
00:00:24,725 --> 00:00:28,865
By the end of this video, you will
read five trends off this one table,

9
00:00:28,865 --> 00:00:31,366
and know why the two ends react the most.

10
00:00:32,016 --> 00:00:34,100
So what is a periodic trend?

11
00:00:34,450 --> 00:00:38,983
A trend is a property that changes
steadily as you move across a period,

12
00:00:38,983 --> 00:00:40,257
or down a group.

13
00:00:40,607 --> 00:00:45,126
Across a period, the nucleus gains
protons, but new electrons join the

14
00:00:45,126 --> 00:00:46,056
same shell.

15
00:00:46,056 --> 00:00:48,442
The pull grows, and atoms shrink.

16
00:00:48,792 --> 00:00:51,881
Down a group, a new
shell is added each time.

17
00:00:51,881 --> 00:00:55,251
The outer electrons sit
farther out, and atoms grow.

18
00:00:55,851 --> 00:00:56,965
Size and pull.

19
00:00:56,965 --> 00:00:59,598
Almost every trend comes from these two.

20
00:01:00,248 --> 00:01:03,352
Let's light up the table,
one trend at a time.

21
00:01:03,352 --> 00:01:04,885
First, atomic radius.

22
00:01:05,235 --> 00:01:10,172
It shrinks across a period, from lithium
at one hundred and fifty two picometres

23
00:01:10,172 --> 00:01:12,002
to fluorine at sixty four.

24
00:01:12,002 --> 00:01:16,445
It grows down a group, up to caesium
at two hundred and sixty two.

25
00:01:16,845 --> 00:01:21,019
Second, ionization enthalpy, the
energy to remove an electron.

26
00:01:21,019 --> 00:01:26,000
It rises across a period, from lithium,
five hundred and twenty, to neon,

27
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:29,194
two thousand and eighty
one kilojoules per mole.

28
00:01:29,194 --> 00:01:30,879
It falls down a group.

29
00:01:31,279 --> 00:01:35,810
Third, electron gain enthalpy, the
energy change when an atom takes in

30
00:01:35,810 --> 00:01:36,793
an electron.

31
00:01:36,793 --> 00:01:41,638
It becomes more negative across a
period, and less negative down a group.

32
00:01:42,038 --> 00:01:45,461
Fourth, electronegativity,
the pull on a shared pair.

33
00:01:45,461 --> 00:01:48,711
It rises across a period,
and falls down a group.

34
00:01:48,711 --> 00:01:50,776
Fluorine is the top, at four.

35
00:01:51,176 --> 00:01:53,075
Fifth, metallic character.

36
00:01:53,075 --> 00:01:55,088
Metals lose electrons easily.

37
00:01:55,088 --> 00:01:59,726
So metallic character falls across
a period, and rises down a group.

38
00:02:00,376 --> 00:02:01,714
Notice the pattern.

39
00:02:01,714 --> 00:02:05,341
Radius and metallic character
grow towards the bottom left.

40
00:02:05,341 --> 00:02:07,978
The other three, towards the top right.

41
00:02:08,378 --> 00:02:09,556
Pause and think.

42
00:02:09,556 --> 00:02:12,544
Which is more metallic,
potassium or magnesium?

43
00:02:15,744 --> 00:02:16,644
Potassium!

44
00:02:16,554 --> 00:02:19,126
It sits further left and further down.

45
00:02:19,126 --> 00:02:21,848
Both moves make an element more metallic.

46
00:02:22,498 --> 00:02:26,267
Now, why are the two ends of
a period the most reactive?

47
00:02:26,267 --> 00:02:27,319
Step by step.

48
00:02:27,669 --> 00:02:28,605
Step one.

49
00:02:28,605 --> 00:02:32,533
On the far left, ionization
enthalpy is the lowest.

50
00:02:32,533 --> 00:02:37,570
Alkali metals lose one electron
easily, and form positive ions, called

51
00:02:37,570 --> 00:02:38,470
cations.

52
00:02:39,130 --> 00:02:40,030
Step two.

53
00:02:40,029 --> 00:02:44,532
On the far right, electron gain
enthalpy is the most negative.

54
00:02:44,532 --> 00:02:49,693
Halogens take an electron easily, and
form negative ions, called anions.

55
00:02:50,443 --> 00:02:51,376
Step three.

56
00:02:51,376 --> 00:02:55,378
In the middle, atoms neither
lose nor gain electrons easily.

57
00:02:55,378 --> 00:02:57,982
So reactivity is lowest in the centre.

58
00:02:58,732 --> 00:02:59,677
Step four.

59
00:02:59,677 --> 00:03:04,466
Noble gases have full shells, and
a positive electron gain enthalpy.

60
00:03:04,466 --> 00:03:06,421
They hardly react at all.

61
00:03:07,171 --> 00:03:08,297
Remember this.

62
00:03:08,297 --> 00:03:09,971
Small atoms pull hard.

63
00:03:09,971 --> 00:03:11,813
Big atoms let go easily.

64
00:03:11,813 --> 00:03:13,971
Every trend follows from that.

65
00:03:14,671 --> 00:03:17,341
Now, one worked example, with oxides.

66
00:03:17,641 --> 00:03:22,854
Using water, show that sodium oxide
is basic and that dichlorine heptoxide

67
00:03:22,854 --> 00:03:23,846
is acidic.

68
00:03:24,346 --> 00:03:27,591
N a two O plus water gives two N a OH.

69
00:03:27,591 --> 00:03:30,092
Sodium hydroxide, a strong base.

70
00:03:30,842 --> 00:03:32,081
Count the atoms.

71
00:03:32,081 --> 00:03:35,333
Sodium, oxygen and
hydrogen: two and two each.

72
00:03:35,333 --> 00:03:36,233
Balanced.

73
00:03:36,849 --> 00:03:40,927
C l two O seven plus water
gives two HC l O four.

74
00:03:40,927 --> 00:03:43,426
Perchloric acid, a strong acid.

75
00:03:44,176 --> 00:03:45,103
Count again.

76
00:03:45,103 --> 00:03:46,719
Chlorine, two and two.

77
00:03:46,719 --> 00:03:48,429
Oxygen, eight and eight.

78
00:03:48,429 --> 00:03:50,045
Hydrogen, two and two.

79
00:03:50,045 --> 00:03:50,945
Balanced.

80
00:03:51,521 --> 00:03:53,691
The base turns red litmus blue.

81
00:03:53,691 --> 00:03:55,862
The acid turns blue litmus red.

82
00:03:56,512 --> 00:03:59,981
Across period three, the
oxides change step by step.

83
00:03:59,981 --> 00:04:03,013
The oxide on the far
left is the most basic.

84
00:04:03,013 --> 00:04:06,045
The one on the far right
is the most acidic.

85
00:04:06,445 --> 00:04:09,620
In the middle, aluminium
oxide is amphoteric.

86
00:04:09,620 --> 00:04:13,911
It behaves as an acid with
bases, and as a base with acids.

87
00:04:14,311 --> 00:04:19,561
Some oxides, like carbon monoxide,
nitric oxide and nitrous oxide, are

88
00:04:19,561 --> 00:04:22,297
neutral: neither acidic nor basic.

89
00:04:22,947 --> 00:04:24,412
Now, the exam trap.

90
00:04:24,762 --> 00:04:29,303
Students think more electrons means a
bigger atom, so the radius must grow

91
00:04:29,303 --> 00:04:30,510
across a period.

92
00:04:30,910 --> 00:04:35,285
But the extra electrons join the same
shell, and the extra protons pull

93
00:04:35,285 --> 00:04:36,185
them in.

94
00:04:36,037 --> 00:04:37,385
The radius shrinks.

95
00:04:38,035 --> 00:04:39,692
Where is this asked?

96
00:04:39,692 --> 00:04:45,266
NEET asks order questions on radius,
ionization enthalpy and metallic character.

97
00:04:45,266 --> 00:04:47,770
JEE Main asks statement questions.

98
00:04:48,370 --> 00:04:49,587
Exam level now.

99
00:04:49,587 --> 00:04:51,919
Which of these orders is correct?

100
00:04:51,919 --> 00:04:53,188
Pause and solve.

101
00:04:58,588 --> 00:04:59,982
The answer is C.

102
00:04:59,982 --> 00:05:02,727
Fluorine, oxygen, nitrogen, carbon.

103
00:05:03,227 --> 00:05:05,896
Electronegativity rises across period two.

104
00:05:05,896 --> 00:05:09,249
Four, three point five,
three, two point five.

105
00:05:09,749 --> 00:05:10,873
A is reversed.

106
00:05:10,873 --> 00:05:14,554
Radius grows down a group,
so fluorine is the smallest.

107
00:05:15,054 --> 00:05:19,908
B is wrong: sodium, further left,
has the lower ionization enthalpy.

108
00:05:19,908 --> 00:05:23,793
D is reversed: metallic
character falls across a period.

109
00:05:24,393 --> 00:05:25,849
Here is your recap.

110
00:05:25,849 --> 00:05:27,346
Screenshot this card.

111
00:05:30,546 --> 00:05:33,383
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

112
00:05:33,783 --> 00:05:34,683
One.

113
00:05:34,310 --> 00:05:37,442
Why does atomic radius
shrink across a period?

114
00:05:38,242 --> 00:05:39,142
Two.

115
00:05:38,771 --> 00:05:42,544
Which is more metallic,
sodium or aluminium, and why?

116
00:05:43,344 --> 00:05:44,244
Three.

117
00:05:43,914 --> 00:05:47,152
Why are the two ends of a
period the most reactive?

118
00:05:48,052 --> 00:05:52,786
Next time: why does boron break the
ionization enthalpy trend, and why

119
00:05:52,786 --> 00:05:54,788
does chlorine beat fluorine?

120
00:05:54,788 --> 00:05:58,750
We find out in exceptions and
anomalous periodic behaviour.
